WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.900
Hey! Can you cover complex analysis? [Chatters
about people requesting complex analysis over the years]

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:12.230
Alright let’s do this. But why
should anybody care about complex analysis?

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:17.439
Well, just like any other field of maths,
it is beautiful, which is enough for pure

00:00:17.439 --> 00:00:23.350
mathematicians, but for applied mathematicians,
physicists, engineers and the like, there

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:30.420
are quite surprisingly lots of profound applications.
It is very useful in solving differential

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:36.550
equations, because the real and imaginary
parts of a complex function satisfy a really

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:44.290
important differential equation. This equation
arises in many areas of physics, like electrostatics,

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:50.230
fluid dynamics, and heat conduction, but even
if you are not solving this particular equation,

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:56.510
for any other DEs, a common trick is to use
Fourier or Laplace transforms, which requires

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:59.720
tools in complex analysis.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:04.940
If that’s not tantalising enough, it is
connected to some popular math topics like

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:10.670
the fundamental theorem of algebra, and the
Riemann hypothesis. Well, if even that is

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:15.840
not attractive enough, it allows you to show
off! No, seriously, how would you evaluate

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:22.260
this integral? Using partial fractions would
be a nightmare, but if we consider its complex

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:28.900
version, integrating on a complex plane, it
becomes massively easier. And actually, these

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:33.970
are only applications that hopefully *most*
of you would find interesting, but obviously

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:40.409
there are more! All I’m trying to say is
that complex analysis is very powerful!

00:01:40.409 --> 00:01:46.621
And so I am going to make a video series about
complex analysis, and this is my plan. The

00:01:46.621 --> 00:01:52.660
first one is this video, a preview of what’s
coming next. Then a crash course on complex

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:59.610
numbers, so that we can all be on the same
page, and then how to visualise complex functions,

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:06.130
and the messy stuff about complex functions.
And then a particular kind of complex function,

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:12.590
the Möbius map, and the slick way of visualising
it using the Riemann sphere. We can’t not

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:18.940
talk about complex differentiation, but it
turns out that if we want to visualise integration,

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:24.540
we also have to know a bit of vector calculus
first. But after all these, we are going to

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:29.959
see *some* of the applications mentioned,
possibly in several videos.

00:02:29.959 --> 00:02:36.280
However, I want to emphasise that it isn’t
like this hasn’t been seen anywhere on YouTube.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:41.560
Welch Labs made a series on complex numbers
and functions, Möbius transformations were

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:47.750
illustrated more than 10 years ago in this
video, Zach Star made a video on complex derivatives,

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:52.840
loads of people have covered vector calculus
before, and actually, this whole series will

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:59.430
be very much from this amazing book called
“Visual Complex Analysis” by Tristan Needham.

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:05.250
So think of this series as a more convenient
compilation and *my* take and animation on

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:11.600
this subject. However, a little note that
might be disappointing to the purists. This

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:17.870
video focuses much more on visual insights,
and the rigour is not prioritised in the arguments.

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:23.000
By all means, make the arguments in the videos
more rigorous! But in any case, I hope you

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.530
will enjoy the series ahead!

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:31.030
Thanks to these patrons, and as always, like
and subscribe with notifications on, comment

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:50.329
and share the video as well! See you next
time!

